---
import Project from '../layouts/Project.astro';
import Section from '../components/Section.astro';
import Panel from '../components/Panel.astro';
import Badge from '../components/Badge.astro';
import { project } from '../data/projects';
const p = project('src-control');
const repo = p.repo;
const desktop = `${repo}/tree/main/apps/desktop`;
const forgeAdr = `${repo}/blob/main/docs/adr/0058-sc-forge-gitea-compatible-git-forge.md`;
const gitBucketAdr = `${repo}/blob/main/docs/adr/0060-git-carried-bucket-remotes.md`;
const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What is src-control?',
    answer: 'src-control is open-source version control built for fleets of AI agents. It combines copy-on-write virtual worktrees, a persistent collaborative VCS, encrypted paths and branches, signed provenance, Git interoperability, a self-hosted Git forge called sc-forge, and a native desktop repository browser.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is src-control used in production?',
    answer: 'One part is. Codento Engine runs sc-forge, the Gitea-compatible Git forge in src-control, as the Git host for its project repositories. The in-memory worktrees, committed secrets and encrypted paths are not in production use there, and src-control has no production users outside Codento Engine.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does src-control write agent worktrees to disk?',
    answer: 'With spill disabled, virtual worktrees stay in memory. With spill enabled, the coldest blobs go to a temporary directory on disk that src-control removes afterwards. A tool that requires real files can receive an explicitly materialized temporary checkout, which src-control also cleans up.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does src-control replace Git?',
    answer: 'No. src-control supports bidirectional Git interoperability, including import, export, and synchronization with local or hosted Git remotes.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does src-control have a desktop application?',
    answer: 'Yes. Phase 35 adds a native Tauri v2 desktop browser for .sc repositories. It reads the object model directly and shows refs, snapshot history and provenance, public trees, and first-parent diffs. This first slice is deliberately read-only and keyless.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is src-control ready for production secret management?',
    answer: 'No. src-control is pre-1.0 and its cryptography has not had an independent audit. Do not trust production secrets to it yet.',
  },
];
const docs = [
  { label: 'README', href: `${repo}#readme` },
  { label: 'Architecture', href: `${repo}/blob/main/ARCHITECTURE.md` },
  { label: 'ADRs', href: `${repo}/tree/main/docs/adr` },
  { label: 'Threat model', href: `${repo}/blob/main/docs/THREAT-MODEL.md` },
  { label: 'Security policy', href: `${repo}/blob/main/SECURITY.md` },
  { label: 'Forge operations', href: `${repo}/blob/main/docs/FORGE-OPERATIONS.md` },
  { label: 'Desktop app', href: desktop },
  { label: 'Contributing', href: `${repo}/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md` },
  { label: 'License', href: `${repo}/blob/main/LICENSE` },
];
---
<Project
  project={p}
  title="In-Memory Git Worktrees for Parallel AI Agents | src-control"
  description="Fork in-memory worktrees for parallel AI agents, collaborate through encrypted branches, sync with Git, and host repositories on sc-forge, a self-hosted Git forge."
  heading="Version control and a Git forge for fleets of AI agents."
  softwareDescription="Open-source version control for parallel AI agent worktrees, encrypted collaboration, signed provenance, Git interoperability, a self-hosted Git forge, and native desktop repository browsing."
  programmingLanguage="Rust"
  faqs={faqs}
  docs={docs}
>
  <Fragment slot="lead">
    src-control forks copy-on-write worktrees in memory for parallel agents,
    keeps secrets and private branches encrypted in the repository, and syncs
    with Git in both directions. Its Git forge, sc-forge, hosts repositories
    over smart HTTP with a Gitea-compatible API subset.
  </Fragment>

  <Fragment slot="status">
    <div><dt>Maturity</dt><dd>Pre-1.0. Phases 1 to 39 (including the P37 partial-clone series, 37a to 37d) are implemented and tested.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Latest phase</dt><dd>P39, Git-carried bucket remotes (<code>sc+git::</code>, <a href={gitBucketAdr}>ADR-0060</a>).</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Production use</dt><dd>sc-forge is the Git host for Codento Engine's project repositories. There are no other production users.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Not yet done</dt><dd>The cryptography has had no independent audit. Do not trust production secrets to it yet.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Rust. The desktop app uses Tauri v2 and TypeScript.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>License</dt><dd>Apache 2.0</dd></div>
  </Fragment>

  <Section id="quickstart" title="Quickstart" note="The commands build and run with cargo.">
    <p>
      Clone <a href={repo}>the repository</a> and run these from its root. The
      demo forks parallel worktrees off one snapshot, runs against each and
      tears them down. The last command is a check for leftover artifacts. It
      lists the <code>scl-*</code> entries in the temp directory and the
      project's files before and after a demo run, and fails if either list
      changed. That run has spill enabled, so blobs can reach a temporary
      spill directory while it runs. The repository has 28 end-to-end demos
      in <code>demo/</code>.
    </p>
    <Panel title="shell" copy>
<pre><code><span class="comment"># fork 4 parallel in-RAM worktrees, run and check out against each</span>
$ cargo run --bin sc -- demo --agents 4

<span class="comment"># force the bounded budget: LRU eviction + spill, auto-cleaned</span>
$ cargo run --bin sc -- demo --agents 6 --budget-mb 4 --spill

<span class="comment"># leftover-artifact check (compares temp dir entries and project files before/after)</span>
$ bash demo/run_demo.sh</code></pre>
    </Panel>
  </Section>

  <Section id="features" title="Features" note="A checkout assumes one developer, one directory and one disk. Agent fleets want dozens of worktrees at once, forked in milliseconds and gone when the run ends.">
    <div class="grid-2">
      <div class="card feature feature--wide">
        <div class="badges"><Badge kind="prod" /></div>
        <h3>sc-forge, a self-hosted Git host</h3>
        <p>
          sc-forge serves bare Git over smart HTTP and a recorded subset of the
          Gitea API: repositories, pull requests, reviews, statuses and branch
          protection. It adds protected merges and ops tooling. All 24 recorded
          scenarios and six first-consumer acceptance scenarios pass.
        </p>
        <p>
          Codento Engine runs sc-forge in production as the Git host for its
          project repositories, where it replaced Gitea. This is the only part
          of src-control in production use.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>In-memory worktrees</h3>
        <p>Fork N copy-on-write worktrees off one snapshot. With spill disabled, content stays in RAM and reaches disk only on an explicit checkout.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>Agent workspaces</h3>
        <p><code>sc work</code> forks workspaces, runs a command in each and harvests the results. <code>sc ws</code> sessions can be named and run concurrently.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>Committed secrets</h3>
        <p>Environment variables and keys live in repository state, encrypted at rest and in transit, and are decrypted only in an authorized execution context.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>Private branches</h3>
        <p>Seal a branch's content, paths and messages to a recipient set, then publish it in one atomic move.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>Signed provenance</h3>
        <p>Ed25519 commit signing with verification of trusted signatures, and sealed agent-session transcripts attached to commits.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>Git interoperability</h3>
        <p>Import from Git, export back to Git, or sync in both directions with a local or hosted Git remote.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>Transports and partial clone</h3>
        <p>SSH and HTTP(S) transports with in-binary TLS, bucket WAL remotes (including <code>sc+git::</code> over any Git host), and lazy partial clones.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card feature">
        <h3>Desktop browser</h3>
        <p>A read-only, keyless Tauri v2 app for refs, snapshot history, provenance, public trees and diffs.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section id="how-it-works" title="How it works" note="A snapshot-and-tag model inspired by Jujutsu.">
    <Panel title="everyday commands">
<pre><code><span class="comment"># agent workspaces: fork N workspaces, run a command in each, harvest results</span>
$ sc work --agents 4 -- cargo test

<span class="comment"># seal an agent-session transcript and attach it to a commit, signed</span>
$ sc transcript attach HEAD session.json --sign

<span class="comment"># stage an embargoed fix on a private branch (content, paths and messages
# all sealed to the recipient set), then make it public in one atomic move</span>
$ sc branch hotfix --private --to alice
$ sc branch grant hotfix --to bob
$ sc branch publish hotfix

<span class="comment"># interoperate with Git: import a repo's HEAD, export history back,
# or use a Git repo (local or GitHub over https/ssh) as a remote</span>
$ sc import --repo /path/to/git/repo
$ sc export --to /path/to/git/repo
$ sc clone https://github.com/org/repo.git ./mirror

<span class="comment"># open a native .sc repository browser (read-only and keyless)</span>
$ cd apps/desktop
$ npm ci &amp;&amp; npm run tauri dev</code></pre>
    </Panel>
    <dl class="rows">
      <div>
        <dt>Copy-on-write worktrees</dt>
        <dd>A worktree is a copy-on-write overlay over an immutable base snapshot. Forking N agents costs O(overlay), not O(repo). Base blobs are shared, never copied.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Disk only on request</dt>
        <dd>
          With spill disabled, content lives in RAM and touches disk only on
          an explicit checkout. There is no FUSE mount and no kernel
          extension. Tools that need real files, such as compilers and test
          runners, get a materialized temporary checkout that src-control
          cleans up.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Bounded memory</dt>
        <dd>A blob budget with LRU eviction bounds memory use. Without spill, an insert over the budget fails. With spill, the coldest blobs go to a content-addressed temporary directory on disk, which src-control removes afterwards.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Durable repositories</dt>
        <dd>
          <code>.sc/</code> repositories have branches, merge with conflict
          tooling (<code>sc conflicts</code> and <code>sc resolve</code>),
          history editing (cherry-pick, rebase, amend, undo), sparse
          checkouts, accidental-secret scanning, per-path encryption,
          packfiles and garbage collection.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Private branches</dt>
        <dd>
          A private branch seals every object it introduces (snapshots, trees
          and blobs) with randomized per-object encryption. Non-recipients
          see no content, file paths, messages or DAG shape. A grant is an
          O(1) key wrap. Revocation rewraps keys without writing plaintext to
          the object store. <code>sc branch publish</code> replays the sealed
          history into public space in one atomic ref move that
          <code>sc undo</code> can reverse, and reruns the secret scanner at
          the boundary.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Provenance</dt>
        <dd>
          src-control supports Ed25519 signing of commits and verification
          of trusted signatures with <code>sc verify</code>. New commits
          created by amend, rebase or merge start unsigned. Sealed
          agent-session transcripts attach to commits.
          Plaintext never enters the object store, and a keyless clone gets
          ciphertext only.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Transports</dt>
        <dd>
          sc-native transports run over SSH and HTTP(S), with in-binary TLS
          (<code>sc+https://</code>), token-based access control with expiry
          and rotation, connection caps and session timeouts. Streaming packs
          move multi-GiB repositories in bounded RAM. Bucket WAL remotes work
          over <code>sc+wal://</code>, <code>sc+s3://</code> and
          <code>sc+git::&lt;git-url&gt;</code>, which stores opaque
          <code>refs/sc/*</code> entries in any Git host so private branches,
          protected files and committed secrets survive a plain Git host, with
          store compaction and <code>sc serve --store</code> support. Partial
          clones fetch lazily, and merge, cherry-pick, rebase and agent
          sessions work on a partial clone.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Git interoperability</dt>
        <dd>
          Git interop goes through <code>gix</code>, isolated in one crate.
          src-control imports a repository's HEAD in-process, exports history
          back to Git commits, and syncs in both directions with a local or
          hosted Git remote, including GitHub over https or ssh.
        </dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Desktop browser</dt>
        <dd>
          The Tauri v2 desktop app reads <code>.sc</code> repositories
          directly through the Rust crates, not through CLI output or a Git
          export. It browses local and remote-tracking refs, the all-parent
          snapshot DAG, signature and transcript provenance, public trees and
          first-parent diffs.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
  </Section>

  <Section id="security" title="Security and limits" note="src-control is pre-1.0. Read this before you rely on it.">
    <dl class="rows">
      <div>
        <dt>No independent audit</dt>
        <dd>The cryptography has not had an independent audit. Do not trust production secrets to src-control yet. The near-term promise is parallel agent workspaces and provenance, not production secret management.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Scope of production use</dt>
        <dd>Production use at Codento Engine covers sc-forge only. The in-memory worktrees, committed secrets and encrypted paths are not in production use there, and no one outside Codento Engine runs src-control in production.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>TLS trust</dt>
        <dd><code>sc+https://</code> trust defaults to first-connection (TOFU) pinning. CA validation is an option, and <code>sc tls</code> manages pins.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Revocation</dt>
        <dd>Revoking a private-branch recipient protects what is sealed afterwards. It cannot erase what they already fetched.</dd>
      </div>
      <div>
        <dt>Desktop app</dt>
        <dd>
          The desktop browser is read-only and keyless. Protected files cross
          IPC only as locked states, private branches remain opaque at their
          public manifests, and the WebView has no general filesystem, shell,
          URL, process or object-store command.
        </dd>
      </div>
    </dl>
    <p class="note">
      The <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/docs/THREAT-MODEL.md`}>threat model</a>
      and the <a href={`${repo}/blob/main/SECURITY.md`}>security policy</a>
      have the details.
    </p>
  </Section>

  <Section id="changes" title="Recent changes" note="November 2026">
    <div class="changes">
      <h3>November 2026</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Phase 39 (<a href={gitBucketAdr}>ADR-0060</a>, #369 to #374) added
          <code>sc+git::</code> bucket remotes, carrying private branches,
          protected files and committed secrets through any Git host
          (validated against GitHub), with store compaction and
          <code>sc serve --store</code> support.
        </li>
        <li>
          sc-forge (P38) runs in production as the Git host for Codento
          Engine's project repositories. Engine builds the sc-forge image from
          a pinned src-control commit and deploys it by digest. The design,
          <a href={forgeAdr}>ADR-0058</a>, was accepted on 25 October 2026.
        </li>
        <li>
          On 20 November a security fix round merged (#360 to #368): fuzzing
          of untrusted decoders, serve hardening, and fixes for a checkout
          escape, recipient sealing and secret environment injection; followed
          on 22 November by dependency security updates (#378, #379).
        </li>
      </ul>
      <h3>Since July 2026</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Phase 36 added bucket WAL remotes over <code>sc+wal://</code> and <code>sc+s3://</code>.</li>
        <li>P37a to P37d added lazy partial clones: want-by-id fetch, presence-based partial clones, and merge, cherry-pick, rebase and agent sessions on a partial clone.</li>
        <li><code>sc ws</code> sessions can be named and run concurrently.</li>
        <li><code>sc tls</code> manages TLS pins, and <code>sc+https://</code> can validate against a CA.</li>
        <li>Tokens expire and rotate, and <code>sc rewrap</code> gained <code>--rotate</code>.</li>
        <li>Clones can filter by blob size, and keys get owner-only ACLs on Windows.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </Section>
</Project>
